"media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:23:45Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "14. Volcanoes, in the popular imagination, are synonymous with heat — molten rock, towering plumes of ash, rivers of lava. But there is a type of geological feature that erupts with dramatic force yet remains entirely cold. Instead of magma, these structures expel mud, water, and gases — driven by the pressure of deep hydrocarbon deposits beneath the surface rather than by heat. They can erupt suddenly and violently, launching debris and cold slurry high into the air, before returning to their low, rumbling state. One country sits atop such an extraordinary concentration of these features that it is home to roughly half of all known examples on earth.\nId X (the country) and Y (the type of geological feature)", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:24:03Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Mud Volcanoes ?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-29T16:24:09Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "iceland?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]